Cambodian Red Cross Donates to Stung Sen Flood Victims
(Kampong Thom): The Cambodian Red Cross has donated to 353 families affected by the Stung Seng floods. CRC's donation was made through Ms. Khieu Sina Chea Chanto, Honorary Vice President of CRC Kampong Thom Branch.
The CRC's donation was given to the people living in Prasat Sambo district with the participation of Ms. Khieu Sina Chea Chanto, Mr. Sok Lou, Provincial Governor and Chairman of the Cambodian Red Cross Commission, Kampong Thom Branch.
Ms. Khieu Sina called on people to be more vigilant, protect young children and take care of their health to avoid various diseases, especially eating and drinking water must be hygienic.
"Everyone can not know in advance or predict the situation of natural disasters, but the important thing now is to help people when they are in need like this," she said.
Mr. Sok Lou also told the provincial Department of Agriculture to prepare enough rice seeds to provide people for replanting after the flood. He added that in the future, we need to help people with food and daily necessities.
"We have to be ready to help them when the water recedes, such as rice, other crops, so that people can grow rice and damaged crops again," he said. The provincial health department must prepare medicine to help treat people when there are other diseases caused by floods while the water continues to rise.
On that occasion, Ms. Khieu Sina and Mr. Sok Lou confirmed in front of the people that under all circumstances, especially when the people are facing difficulties, Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian Red Cross, always consider helping to solve and Stay with the well-being of the people, even if he did not visit in person, send a representative or team to help like now.
Mr. Uth Vannara, Governor of Prasat Sambo District, said that the floods in Prasat Sambo district affected 5 communes and affected 634 families in the flood of 16,561 hectares of rice fields, which reached 1,550 hectares. Hectares damaged.
