JAKARTA: Grab, Southeast Asia's leading mobile app maker, contributed $ 5.8 billion to the Southeast Asian economy as of March 2019. This is according to its first report on social impacts released today from Jakarta.
This contribution is based on the revenue of driving partners, transportation services, merchants and agents, and from the sales collected through Grab's platform.
The report also states that as many as 9 million micro-entrepreneurs, such as drivers, are using their technology across the region by the end of August 2019, meaning that one in 70 Southeast Asia is monetized through Grab.
As of March 2018, that number was only 2.6 million, thus showing a significant increase in Grab technology usage in 339 cities across eight countries in Southeast Asia. .
Grab's Social Impact Report also marks the launch of a new social impact program called Grab for Good, which aims to benefit people in Southeast Asia. Great on technology, raising awareness of technology and digital services.
This new initiative will allow more people to become part of the fast-growing digital economy, as well as have more options and opportunities to improve their lives. Taking advantage of the platform technology and its partnership, Grab has set some ambitious goals for the Grab for Good program, which will be achieved by 2025.
This will benefit an additional 5 million micro-entrepreneurs and small business owners, as well as create a workforce ready for the future by training 20,000 students through the initiative. On technological intelligence.
"If the private sector actively develops programs for local communities, technology can expand the reach of many people, and learning new skills can greatly improve the lives of more people," he said. Fast forward to Southeast Asia. ”
