(Kampong Chhnang): The specialized police force of Kampong Chhnang Provincial Police Commission stopped a truck transporting illegal animals of "Kunthea Reaksmey Pich" Company and handed it over to the Provincial Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries for a fine. This was confirmed by Mr. Ngin Hun, Director of the Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries of Kampong Chhnang Province, told Fresh News this morning, September 24, 2019.
Mr. Ngin Hun stated that one vehicle exceeded the legal limit, which required only 4,000 chickens, but his officials found up to 5,000 live chickens. According to the director of the Department of Agriculture, for this crime, a fine of 10 million to 15 million riels is required.
Mr. Ngin Hun said that this morning, company representatives presented the law to his professional officers, who had indeed stolen more than the legal limit. He said the truck imported live chickens from Thailand to Phnom Penh for distribution.
According to a report from Kampong Chhnang province, on the afternoon of September 23, 2019, there were two cars of "Kunthea Reaksmey Pich" company, but they turned their heads back when they found out that one of their cars Was detained by the authorities.
According to the report, the trucks carrying live chickens were shipped out of Thailand through the Boeung Chhnang corridor in Battambang province's Kamrieng district, and most of them were overloaded, with some trucks carrying more than half of them. .
In fact, at more than 2 pm on September 18, 2019, the police force of Kampong Chhnang province also stopped a car of "Kunthea Reaksmey Pich" company at Trach market in Samaki Meanchey district, Kampong Chhnang province. This was confirmed by Major General Khov Ly, Provincial Police Commissioner.
According to the authorities, the vehicle was a truck carrying nearly 10,000 live chickens, while the law only allowed 4,000.
It should be noted that after some protests against the suspension of the import of live chickens from neighboring countries, especially Thailand, Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries came out to explain more about this issue, stating that the suspension. The import of live chickens is done according to the wishes of local farmers, while the principle of importing them has increased too much.
Mr. Veng Sakhon said that the actual demand for chickens is only 17,000 per day, and if imported up to 45,000 chickens per day, that will have a negative impact on domestic development. In addition, the money received from the World Bank and ADB projects to train people does not seem to be of any benefit.
