Tao William transformed from a lazy student on the phone to become an A-level student
Tao William is a young man who put his heart and soul into hard work until he got an A in the high school diploma exam in 2019, while previously he was a lazy student playing only games on the phone. It is admirable that this young man has become such a role model.
Tao Williams is a former student of Natsalin School, Stung Meanchey Branch, and was one of the 443 A-level students in the August 19, 2019 exam, with a total of 117,043 candidates. He was born in Trea Village, Sangkat Steung Meanchey, Khan Meanchey, Phnom Penh.
On the afternoon of September 23, 2019, Wesleyan Education Group organized an award ceremony for 6 teachers, students, A-level winners for the 2018-2019 academic year. Young Tao William shared his successful experience in front of many students.
Tao Williams said that he used to be a student who only played with the phone and did not study hard. But when he entered the 12th grade, he tried to correct this bad habit and put in a clear study plan because he had the ambition to get good grades in order to easily get a scholarship to continue his studies at universities both in the country and abroad.
Tao Williams used three methods to achieve his goal. The young man added that the first is the ambition to get good grades, the second is to find a way to learn is to study the basic formulas of the subject and third is to set yourself a lot of exercises before touching the phone.
"Some people know by writing and remembering, but I like to listen to others, such as professionals and the Khmer language," Tao Williams said. For math, physics, chemistry, we desperately need to remember its basic formula.
Mr. Siv Sam Oeun, a teacher at Stung Meanchey branch of the North Line School, gave an interview to Fresh News about the study history of William students. He said that William was a patient student, that he had changed his habit of playing a lot of mobile games to study hard.
Mr. Siv Sam Oeun also closely followed William's study. He said that in the last three months, William has worked hard to study and practice the exercises that the teacher has assigned to him, but he does not take any non-essential subjects in the exam because he does not have time to study. Important.
"William is a good, patient student, which means he is good at trying, he determines his studies properly, he knows how to study," he said. He mainly focuses on the core subjects of the exam, such as mathematics, chemistry, biology, Khmer language and history. "Usually William comes to school on time. He is a student. He respects teachers. He respects time. He loves his friends. He always helps his friends who are weak and he has his own club."
He added that when William was in 10th or 11th grade, during his free time, he dialed the phone, but at the time of the 12th year, he promised himself to stop holding the phone and stop playing games on the phone. He set the goal in his study that if he touches the phone until he has done three or four exercises or a lot of exercise, then playing the phone, he has time to relax and relax the brain in particular. Play the phone to see lessons related to the study.
At the same time, Ms. Oung Srey, the mother of Tao William, praised her children for their hard work and success in school. She says her children are diligent, even though they are busy studying, they do not forget to help with the housework.
She adds that as a businessman, she has a lot of work to do and her children still take time to help her with her work. Another thing she admires about William is not being able to hang out and learning English well for 10 years in a row.
Mr. Pech Bolen, President of Wesleyan Education Group, congratulated all the students who passed the exam. He considered that this is the success of the school that has contributed to the promotion of education in Cambodia.
Mr. Pech Bolen praised William and the other five students who studied at the branches for their excellent grades. He urged them to continue their efforts to learn more for the future.
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Chea Pisey, Governor of Russey Keo District and a member of the Secretariat of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, instructed all officials and district law enforcement officers to take turns to maintain security at other fun places. Especially in the pagodas during the Pchum Ben festival to the people before he led the district delegation to bring donations to support and visit the army and families defending the territory in the front battle.
Mr. Chea Pisey's message was made during his meeting to support the budget and distribute drinks to many district officials and law enforcement forces on the occasion of the 10th Pchum Ben Ceremony at Khan Hall on the afternoon of September 23, 2019. This.
