Alumni Association of Cambodia urges people, students to love and protect natural resources by replanting trees
(Tbong Khmum): Mr. Mey Sithon, President of the Alumni Association of Cambodia, said that natural resources are very important, which requires participation in the development, reforestation, love of natural resources, especially the protection and Conserve the environment together.
This statement was made on the occasion of Mr. Mesithon leading a team to distribute study materials and plant seedlings at Wat Chhouk in Chhouk village, Chhouk commune, Krouch Chhmar district, Tbong Khmum province on September 21, 2019. The ceremony was also attended by Mr. Peang Narith, Deputy Governor of Tbong Khmum.
Mr. Mey Sithon emphasized that in relation to the activities of the family, the association has developed a clear plan for the development of the association, such as helping the poor, the vulnerable and the vulnerable, especially the participation of society as part of the driving force. Of participating in the development of the nation.
He stated that the participation in planting trees now aims to promote development in society through participation in loving the environment and to show people as well as students to understand more to participate in protecting, loving, And care for the environment in their communities.
"In particular, the joint responsibility for the protection and conservation of our district environment as climate change threatens day by day." This is an additional confirmation of the Alumni Association of Cambodia.
On that occasion, Mr. Peang Narith expressed his warm appreciation to the Cambodian Alumni Association for planning to plant seedlings at the grassroots level, because planting seedlings will benefit us as well. Shades for students to sit and learn, especially to set a good example to help inspire students to study outside the education system.
He also called on people in the community as well as throughout Tbong Khmum province to participate in the spirit of forest protection and tree planting, because the forest is an important natural resource not only to support people and animals. No, it is also beautiful, especially providing accommodation for tourists and a legacy for future generations.
It should be noted that the Alumni Association of Cambodia has participated in planting more than 1,200 seedlings of Kranhong Beng and Thnong, which were planted at secondary schools, health centers and pagodas in Chhouk commune and brought Distribute study materials to more than 400 students and give gifts to teachers and uncles, aunts and local authorities.
Last Friday, Chinese officials canceled a visit to a farm in the US states of Montana and Nebraska after concluding two days of talks in Washington as the visit took place this week. This has also raised concerns about Sino-US trade talks.
Now, Han Jun has said that the preparatory talks with the US side last week went well in preparation for the opening of a new round of high-level talks on trade wars in early October. Coming soon. According to Han Jun, the two sides have had thorough and frank discussions and have achieved good results in agriculture.
