WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The United States and Canada have lost nearly 3 billion birds since the 1970s, according to a new study. But the remarkable loss indicates that global biodiversity is in crisis, mainly due to climate change. This was quoted by Sputnik on Saturday evening, September 21, 2019.
The study, conducted by scientists from seven research institutes in the United States and Canada, was published in the journal Science this week. The study found that more than 300 species of birds in the United States and Canada have declined 29 percent, or nearly 3 billion, in nearly 50 years.
In recent days, the whole world is debating the report of scientists who have warned that the earth will warm more than expected if the level of pollution is the same every day. This is not reduced. This has raised concerns among countries and called on all countries to reconsider their efforts to protect the environment as much as possible.
