Samdech Heng Samrin Leads a High-Level Delegation to the Asia-Europe Parliamentary Assembly in Kazakhstan
(Phnom Penh): Samdech Akka Moha Ponhea Chakrei Heng Samrin, President of the National Assembly of Cambodia, this morning, September 22, 2019, led a high-level delegation of the National Assembly to attend the 4th Asia-Europe Parliamentary Presidential Meeting in the Republic of Kazakhstan.
The 4th Asia-Europe Parliamentary Assembly Level Meeting was held under the theme "Higher Excellence for Eurasia: Dialogue on Confidence and Partnership" from 23 to 25 September 2019, with a delegation of parliamentary leaders. In Asia and Europe, about 60 countries participate, as well as the presence of international organizations and inter-parliamentary organizations.
The Asia-Europe Parliamentary Assembly Meeting, initiated by the Duma of the Russian Federation and the National Assembly of the Republic of Korea, is also an important inter-parliamentary forum for discussions on the topic of cooperation between the representatives of the peoples of Asia and Europe. Exchange views on safeguarding the interests of sustainable development and social welfare in the region as well as the world.
During the meeting, Samdech Akka Moha Ponhea Chakrei Heng Samrin is scheduled to meet with the National Assembly Presidents of other countries to discuss the expansion of parliamentary diplomacy, as well as to strengthen cooperation and friendship between the Kingdom of Cambodia and other countries. Friends in both bilateral and multilateral frameworks.
The first meeting of the Asia-Europe Parliamentary Assembly was held in Russia in 2016, the second in the Republic of Korea in 2017 and the third in Turkey in 2018. Samdech Akka Moha Ponhea Chakrei Heng Samrin has attended the first meeting in Russia and the second in the Republic of Korea.
In 1958, Aramco's crude oil production exceeded 1 million barrels per day. By 1962, the company had achieved another milestone: 5 billion barrels of crude oil production.
By 1973, the Saudi government held a 25% stake in Aramco and bought up to 60% the following year. It was not until 1980 that the Saudi government was able to take full control of the giants. A year after the company was completely taken over by the state, it has evolved from an oil producer and exporter to a mixed oil company.
Aramco has its own airport in the United States for its staff, as well as a number of passenger planes and helicopters.
