Find out: Why is the US hiding its oil reserves in the tunnels?
(Foreign): After the recent attack on two large oil refineries of the oil giant Aramko in the eastern part of Saudi Arabia, global oil prices have soared. One level. As a precautionary measure, President Donald Trump has allowed the United States to release its underground oil reserves to prevent oil prices from rising too much.
All member states of the International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) must maintain a supply of self-sufficient oil for at least 90 days, while the United States is the world's largest stockpile of emergency oil.
* Why was a reserve oil plan developed?
US politicians first came up with the idea of stockpiling oil in the early 1970s after the United States was hit by oil sanctions by Middle Eastern countries, which caused oil prices to skyrocket around the world. Mr.
Such a method of storing oil underground is considered cheaper and safer than storing it on the surface because the chemical composition of the salt and the geological pressure of the groundwater will prevent the oil from leaking out. Yes.
According to the US Energy Information Administration, Americans consume an average of 20.5 million barrels of oil per day, which means that those stored underground oil can supply the American people for 31 days. .
* How does the oil storage cave work?
Under a 1975 law signed by former US President Gerald Ford, the president could allow the release of those reserves in the most urgent need of energy.
The conditions for the release of oil determined that only a small amount of oil could be transported from the caves every day. Therefore, it means that even if there is an order from the presidential authority to release oil, it will take up to two weeks to meet the needs of the international market.
In addition, the oil stored in these caves is not completely refined. Therefore, more time and energy is needed to turn those fuels into fuel suitable for use in cars, ships and aircraft. "It's a little too early to talk about reliance on oil reserves after the attack on Saudi Arabia," Energy Secretary Rick Perry told CNBC.
Despite the many interpretations, the question remains as to why the United States is retaining so much reserves while its energy production is on the rise. Some have even suggested that it should be completely eliminated. According to a 2014 report by the Government Accounting Bureau (GAO), the move could push down oil prices for US consumers.
