Ministry of Health and related partners jointly launch a strategy to eliminate hepatitis B and C by 2030
(Phnom Penh): The Minister of Health, Prof. Mam Bunheng, stated that the Ministry of Health has already prepared a plan and set work guidelines in line with the World Health Organization (WHO) guidelines for the elimination of hepatitis B and C in the world. By 2030.
According to the World Health Organization, if the goal of eliminating hepatitis B and C is achieved, it will prevent 7.1 million deaths from the disease worldwide.
This statement was made by Minister Mam Bunheng on the occasion of chairing a roundtable discussion on hepatitis C on September 20, 2019 at the University of Health Sciences.
Minister Mam Bunheng continued, "We have been preparing and launching a strategic plan for the fight against viral hepatitis and have established a technical working group to eradicate viral hepatitis." "Therefore, the results of today's roundtable discussion will be an important input for strategic planning for the elimination of viral hepatitis."
The World Health Organization (WHO) has identified three key strategies for implementation, including:
1: Prevention of transmission must be done to reach hard-to-reach people.
2. The presence of new, highly effective drugs for the treatment of hepatitis B and C provides an opportunity to begin, strengthen and expand testing and treatment.
Minister Mam Bunheng stated, "We can do these things by expanding to the grassroots." In addition, the Royal Government under the wise and wise leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, Cambodia is peaceful and has developed in all areas, especially the health sector has achieved many important achievements.
In fact, we have achieved the Millennium Development Goals by reducing infant and child mortality, reducing morbidity and mortality. These achievements have always been recognized nationally and internationally, especially by the United Nations and the World Health Organization.
