(Phnom Penh): The General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia held a dissemination workshop on customs and tax incentives for small and medium enterprises on the morning of September 20, 2019. The workshop was chaired by Dr. Kun Nhim, a Royal Government delegate in charge of the Director General of the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia.
The workshop was also attended by representatives of the General Department of Taxation, the General Department of Small and Medium Enterprises and Handicrafts, the Chamber of Commerce (Customs Working Group for Small and Medium Enterprises), the Alliance of Small and Medium Enterprises Associations, the Young Entrepreneurs Association. Children and small and medium enterprises located in Phnom Penh, a total of more than 100 participants.
Speaking on the occasion, Dr. Kun Nhim emphasized that small and medium enterprises are the backbone of the economy and a strong driving force for growth and job creation.
In this context, the Royal Government has set out a number of measures to support this sector, as stated in the policy and strategic documents: First, the development policy Cambodia Industrial Sector 2015-2025, Second: Rectangular Strategy, Phase 4 and Third: Revenue Collection Strategy 2019-2023.
From 2001 to 2019, the Royal Government has put in place measures to support the agricultural sector and small and medium enterprises, including lowering tariffs, special tax rates, imposing value-added taxes on the state and levying taxes. Exports are borne by the state on agricultural products, seeds, crops, livestock, pharmaceuticals, animal feed, agricultural machinery and production raw materials in these sectors.
The workshop was also attended by representatives of the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia and representatives of the General Department of Taxation on the content of sub-decrees and prakas of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, stating the conditions, forms, procedures and mechanisms of Provision and monitoring of incentives for small and medium enterprises in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.
Small and medium enterprises can find more information through the websites of the General Department of Customs and Government of Cambodia and the General Department of Taxation, or can contact the liaison officer at the nearest Customs and Excise branch or tax branch.
Finally, Dr. Kun Nhim urged all owners or representatives of small and medium enterprises to pay attention to the presentation and raise questions as well as suggestions to the two General Departments, including stakeholders. The implementation of this incentive is effective, transparent and in line with the actual situation in Cambodia.
