Anti-Counterfeiting Committee: Authorities must sternly warn individuals who stockpile counterfeit products
(Phnom Penh): General Miech Sophanna, Secretary of State and Chairman of the Anti-Counterfeit Products Committee (CDC), stressed that the authorities must strongly warn individuals who stockpile counterfeit products and close their businesses.
The workshop was attended by speakers from the Ministry of Commerce, General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia, and senior officials of the PEC Committee.
General Miech Sophanna stated that counterfeiting, which is the copying of various brands of products such as clothing, bags, shoes, pharmaceuticals, aircraft and vehicle parts, aircraft, electronics, toys, art, toys and movies, etc., is relevant in some cases. And forgery of trademarks and product names, which infringes on intellectual property rights.
He said most of the counterfeit products were of low quality and contained dangerous chemicals such as lead in the products, which caused the deaths of hundreds of thousands of people and could lead to car accidents, poisoning or drug cases. There is no therapeutic effect.
"In order to fight counterfeit and illegal products effectively, we need to have adequate laws and regulations, have a high level of will and commitment, change consumer attitudes, have strict law enforcement, with the participation of all stakeholders and have a responsibility," he said. "Good cooperation between ministries and institutions in the country and with neighboring countries and countries around the world."
Gen. Miech Sophanna added that the relevant authorities must issue strict instructions and warnings to individuals who stockpile counterfeit products and close their businesses, and to property owners who rent shops or warehouses will be held accountable in accordance with the law. Counterfeit products leave the market and out of the flow of trade and will not be allowed to return to the market.
