Open Letter: US Ambassador Muffy on Mission for Improving Cambodia-US Relations, Not for Rebels
(Phnom Penh): After being absent for almost a year, today the new US Ambassador, Patrick Moffi, has resumed his duties. As the people of Cambodia, I would like to extend a warm welcome to the sublime presence on the territory of Cambodia, which is a land of tragedy due to the crisis of the fires of war, the crisis due to the interference of the host countries.
His Excellency's visit to Cambodia at a time when Cambodia and the United States are at loggerheads over differences of opinion. I sincerely hope and believe that His Excellency's noble mission will be the most important contribution to reconciling the leaks and misunderstandings of the Royal Government of Cambodia and the Government of the United States of America.
I believe that Your Excellency really knows and remembers very well the history of past relations between Cambodia and the United States in the late 1960s and early 1970s. Therefore, I would like to send my high hopes to His Excellency that His noble mission will not make Cambodia and the United States have a bitter history like in the past.
The Cambodian nation, though weak, is not as famous as the Angkorian Empire, but the Cambodian people still have an independent heart and do not like to be pressured or coerced by anyone to do anything. The Cambodian nation, a nation that overcomes all difficulties, also refuses to follow anyone's orders.
Cambodians like sweet words, like softness, until there is a phrase that the ancient Khmer say "hot, shallow, cold, deep". I believe that by showing the depth of the heart and soul of Cambodia, this will help His Excellency to work in harmony with Cambodia and help His Excellency's mission to be successful.
On another issue of misunderstanding between Cambodia and the United States, which I expect in the mission of His Excellency, will be able to close this misunderstanding. Related to the problems of former leaders of the former opposition party in Cambodia. A recent State Department spokesman has repeatedly spoken out, demanding that the Royal Government of Cambodia release Kem Sokha and pave the way for the former CNRP to return to politics.
The misunderstanding of the US State Department spokesman may have been due to the fact that the US did not have an ambassador to Cambodia in the past, which made the correspondence difficult. Therefore, His Excellency's noble presence will make the United States aware of Cambodia's law enforcement.
I also strongly hope that His Excellency's mission will strive to improve Cambodian-US relations and cooperation by working with the legitimate Royal Government. Not working with a group of criminals, Sam Rainsy rebels, who openly declare money to support the army to seize power illegally.
May His Excellency's noble mission be a great success.
Sincerely yours, Van Saray, Cambodians who do not want to see Cambodia-US have a history of suffering like in the past.
