(Phnom Penh): According to the Summit on Waste in Cambodia 2019, held on September 19, 2019, claimed that the whole country has more than 3 million tons of garbage per year.
"Cambodia as a whole produces more than 10,000 tons of waste every day, or more than 3.6 million tons per year," the statement said.
The Cambodia Waste Management Summit 2019 is a gathering of executives from the Cambodian business community, policy makers and relevant departments, as well as environmental organizations and start-ups.
The goal of the summit is to establish dialogue that encourages partnerships between public and private sector venture capitalists, investors and other stakeholders in the sector.
Nget Bun, deputy director of the General Department of Solid Waste at the Ministry of Environment, said that in Cambodia, waste management, especially solid waste, is still not good.
He said that now, as developing countries, solid waste is on the rise, and solid waste management in Cambodia is not about solid waste management, it is just about collecting garbage.
"I can say that it is not a solid waste management, it is just a clean city, because our solid waste management has to start from the establishment to the final stages of abandonment," he said. "Make it safe, not harmful to the environment and health."
"I hope we can contribute to the successful promotion of public-private partnerships on waste management," he added. "We have a lot of opportunities to accomplish this."
Waste management is an issue that needs to be addressed urgently in Cambodia. Strong economic growth, rapid urban expansion and industrial development, along with increasing consumption of the middle- and upper-class population, which has increased in the last two decades To have a significant impact on the amount of waste in Cambodia.
According to the press release, every day, Phnom Penh alone generates between 2,000 and 3,000 tons of waste, of which 600 tons are plastic waste. The growth of this waste includes all kinds of waste, including urban solid waste, industrial waste, hazardous waste, construction waste and waste from building demolition, which causes many challenges.
In general, problems in waste management include lack of proper waste collection, infrastructure, technical treatment, technical staff training, as well as effective management policies. The increasing amount of waste is causing serious health and environmental problems that are often mentioned in local media and social media.
