Cambodia rejects US call for release of former opposition member (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Cambodian government spokesman Phay Siphan claims that the US State Department's call for the release of a former member of the opposition The former opposition party, the Royal Government of Cambodia, could not do that. The denial was discussed at a press conference on the morning of September 19, 2019.
"The order or request to the Royal Government of Cambodia is not possible, we do not have the power to encroach on the court's obligations," Phay Siphan told reporters.
On September 18, 2019, the US State Department, through its spokeswoman Morgan Ortagus, issued a short video release expressing concern over the political situation in Cambodia and urging the Royal Government of Cambodia to release former party activists. Opponents recently arrested.
A spokesman for the Royal Government of Cambodia considered that the US State Department did not yet understand the law and the division of powers in Cambodia. He confirmed that the CNRP was dissolved by the court at the end of 2017, based on facts and law, and according to the law, there are no more CNRP activists.
He added that what is the responsibility of the Royal Government of Cambodia to do this is to comply with court orders, because Cambodia has divided its power into three separate branches: the legislature, the executive and the judiciary. He added that the prime minister's prerogative to request the pardon or commutation of someone's sentence is only after the court proceedings.
"Obliged people of the court, with the facts and the law, the Royal Government has no power to interfere, except to end the case of the court, which is the prerogative of the Prime Minister," he said. "The detainee must go through the court process, and the prime minister's prerogative is when the court's procedural law allows the prime minister to ask for pardon or commutation."
