(Phnom Penh): Preah Kamrong Teng Anh "Jayavarman", who later became the emperor of the great kingdom of Kampuchea, named Jayavarman VII, or Jayavarman VII, ruled most of mainland Southeast Asia. Nearly 1,000 years ago, he left many great words and phrases to educate the next generation of Cambodians.
9: "If the leader is virtuous, the people are happy, but if the leader is not virtuous, the people will be miserable."
At the beginning of the 13th century, during the reign of Jayavarman VII, Cambodia was very large, ruling over present-day Cambodia, Kampuchea Krom, Central Vietnam (formerly the Champa Kingdom), Laos, and present-day Thailand, including part of Cape Malacca to Cape Koh Krak to Burma to the Salween River.
