(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Public Works and Transport (MPWT) on September 15, 2019 issued a press release on the operation to tow sand barges attached to the third pillar of the Prek Mak Bridge in Kandal province. The ministry confirmed that the operation was temporarily suspended, waiting for the water to recede before resuming after using all means but without success.
According to a press release obtained by Fresh News today, on Sunday afternoon, September 8, 2019, an accident occurred when a sand barge was washed away by water. With the pillars of the bridge pillar, the eastern part of Prek Tamak bridge, located in Khsach Kandal and Muk Kampoul districts, Kandal province.
After a thorough discussion with the technical team of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport and a group of bridge construction experts from China, it was confirmed that the above-mentioned sand ferry did not cause serious damage to the piers and structure of the Prek bridge. The whole eye.
Therefore, the working group decided to temporarily suspend the operation of the above ferry and the Ministry of Public Works and Transport will resume operations when the water is relieved.
Speaking to Fresh News on the night of September 14, 2019, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, emphasized that the date for the re-operation has not been confirmed yet because the technical team and Experts from relevant ministries and institutions have to wait for the water to recede before they can continue their work, because at this time the water is very high.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, who has been in charge of the scene since the first day, claimed that the decision to suspend the activities was made on the instructions of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia. He added that Samdech Techo Hun Sen had clearly instructed and strictly forbade the team to use this method to cut the sand ferry in two for fear of endangering the operation team.
According to Mr. Sun Chanthol, another reason for the technical team to decide to suspend this activity is due to the confirmation from the Shanghai company, which is the contractor of the Prek Tamak bridge, came to inspect the scene and claimed that there was no Any damage to the bridge piers or the entire bridge body due to the ferry hit.
Please be informed that this incident happened on the afternoon of September 7, 2019.
