Mr. Neth Pheaktra said: "If the business owner has hidden or sold souvenirs made of ivory and rhinoceros, please immediately hand over to the authorities and if the authorities find and catch will take action against the perpetrators. The law is necessary. ”
According to the Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment, the sale of ivory and rhinoceros sculptures or trading in other forms related to other wildlife is in violation of Article 97 (point 10) and the Law on Protected Areas under Article 59. (Point 5) If found guilty, they will face severe fines and imprisonment.
In addition, the occurrence of these activities on the territory of the Kingdom of Cambodia will have a negative impact on the efforts of the Royal Government of Cambodia as a Member State of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora. Danger as well as damage to Cambodia's reputation on the international stage. "These actions face the strictest enforcement of the law."
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that the issuance of this notice to advise business owners to immediately stop the illegal wildlife trade and strengthen the enforcement of anti-crime law to eliminate all forms of wildlife trafficking. All processing and sales facilities.
It should be noted that in the past, the Cambodian authorities have seized ivory, ivory and ivory sculptures that were smuggled into Cambodia and exported in many cases. According to figures obtained from 2016 until the end of 2018, thousands of ivory and rhinoceroses weighing more than 5 tons were cracked down on by the Cambodian authorities. Apart from that, there were also scorpions and other wild animal bones that were cracked down on by the authorities.
