The following is a brief biography of Sisoulit:
Thongloun Sisoulit is a Lao politician who has been the Prime Minister of Laos since April 2016. He is also the fourth member of the Politburo of the Lao People's Revolutionary Party.
Sisoulit became Deputy Minister of Foreign Affairs of Laos in 1987 and later served as Minister of Labor and Social Welfare and Chairman of the Foreign Affairs Committee of the Lao National Assembly.
From 2001 to 2006, Sisoulit served as Deputy Prime Minister of Laos and Chairman of the State Planning Committee. From 2006 to early 2016, he served as Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Foreign Affairs, and it was not until April 2016 that Thongloun Sisoulit became the Prime Minister of Laos.
Laos is one of the top 10 countries in Southeast Asia. The following is the general situation of this mainland Southeast Asian country:
I. History:
During the Vietnam War, the eastern part of Laos fell under the control of the North Vietnamese army, transporting weapons and food to the army to counter the South Vietnamese army. In response, the United States launched a bombing campaign against North Vietnam against the communist regime. Eventually a series of coups ensued, and a civil war broke out between the Lao government and the Lao PDR communist movement.
In a newspaper published in 1990, human rights activist Vang Pobzeb wrote that Laos had been under Vietnamese rule since December 2, 1975, and had been led by Vietnam in internal and external affairs. Through the 1977 Treaty between Laos and Vietnam, Vietnam not only gave Vietnam direct rights in foreign policy, but also the right to intervene in political and economic affairs at all levels.
In 1979, Laos was asked to end relations with the People's Republic of China, which led to the severing of ties with China, the United States and other countries. In 1979, there were 50,000 Vietnamese troops in Laos, more than 6,000 Vietnamese civilian officials, 1,000 of whom directly intervened in the ministries of Vientiane government.
II. Geographical and climatic conditions
The Lao economy is supported by the International Monetary Fund and other international sources, as well as foreign investment. Tourism is the fastest growing industry in Laos. However, the Lao economy is seriously threatened by the outflow of human resources. According to a 2005 World Bank study, 37% of Lao scholars decide to move abroad.
The official language in Laos is Lao, which belongs to the Tai language family. The French language, which is widely used in government and business, has gradually weakened as English has become more popular.
Theravada Buddhism has a major influence on Lao culture, as evidenced by language, art, literature, and so on. However, some of the original Lao cultures that preceded the advent of Buddhism are still preserved to this day, such as the ken, a national musical instrument that originated from prehistoric times. Please be informed that Laos has two world heritage sites: Luang Prabang and Wat Phu.
Since rice is the staple food in Laos, there are many religious rituals and traditions related to rice cultivation. In fact, Khmer farmers in Luang Prabang often grow a small amount of khao kam rice near the huts as a token of gratitude to their parents who have passed away or planted near the fields to confirm that their parents are There is life.
