General Commission of National Police Holds a Meeting on Experimental Work on Prevention and Prevention of Theft in Phnom Penh
(Phnom Penh): On the morning of August 11, 2019, the General Commission of the National Police held a meeting to test the work of preventing and preventing theft in Phnom Penh.
The meeting was held at the meeting hall of the Criminal Police Department under the chairmanship of General Him Yan and Lieutenant General In Bora, Deputy Commissioner General of the National Police, and was attended by the Director of the Department, Deputy Director of the Department and other relevant police leaders. Also.
According to the report of Major General Suy Sokunthea, Chief of the Secretariat for the Prevention and Suppression of Theft in Phnom Penh, there were 63 crimes in August, but with the efforts of all units Together, they cracked down on 50 cases and arrested 64 suspects, accounting for 79%.
Speaking on the occasion, Lt. Gen. In Bora noted that although the overall crime rate had increased, the crackdown was effective with the close cooperation of all relevant forces. After all, this is also the pride of all forces.
However, Deputy Commissioner General In Bora also reminded the leaders and all relevant police forces to work together to prevent further and reduce all possible crimes in order to maintain security, order and social security. To citizens and tourists.
At the same time, General Him Yan also praised the judicial police force, which has done a good job by cooperating with all relevant professional forces on the prevention and suppression of theft in Phnom Penh.
In addition, Lt. Gen. Neng Chuor, Director of the Criminal Police Department, also instructed all criminal police to update all subjects that in the past or present have been involved in burglary activities in order to introduce educational measures to prevent and monitor these targets. This reactivates.
He added that experts also need to update all subjects (theft, robbery, smoking, drug use) with arrest warrants or search warrants (based on the verdict or final verdict) to identify the target. They are to crack down or seize the escape of individuals or families in order to have a thorough investigation.
