Direct power from two Lao coal-fired power plants with a total capacity of 2,400 megawatts located in the coal mining area of Sekong Province, Laos.
Mr. Phay Siphan affirmed that the plan to add electricity to meet the needs of political stability and peace in Cambodia has brought a lot of economic investment in all areas, and then the situation will be better after the 2018 general election. Increasing this investment activity in the past has affected the demand for electricity to increase more than planned.
Although this is due to the fact that our country's power supply system is still small, we still need to connect with large power supply systems such as the Vietnam supply system or the Thai supply system to an appropriate level to strengthen the balance of the supply system. Our country's electricity as well.
Investment in the above two projects is operational and enables EDC to purchase electricity from both projects, which is a long-term strategic benefit for Cambodia. The benefits that Cambodia can get from purchasing power sources from the above two power generation projects are as follows:
* Get $ 7.7 cents per kilowatt hour, which is cheaper than other coal-fired power plants in Cambodia and has a fixed price unlike imported coal from high-risk international markets. This price is in response to the government's policy to reduce electricity prices in the future.
* The technical and economic terms of both agreements are freer than previous agreements.
* Electricity from both sources may help reduce the purchase of expensive electricity from sources currently imported from Vietnam and Thailand.
* The new power source from the above two projects ensures a stable supply to the national system, especially in the dry season, when most of our hydropower stations do not have much production capacity, can only operate about 30% of Its production capacity.
* Bring understanding and training to increase the skills of EDC workers through study at the station.
* No environmental impact in Cambodia.
