With this new feature, passengers will be able to share details of their ride and current location, and passengers can access the Help Center as well as send notification messages to anyone for communication. Their urgency, too.
The new app comes after Grab introduced a similar mobile app feature for its driver partners earlier this year, in which partners can share their location or contact the app directly from the app. They.
Hashim Alkaff, Grab Cambodia Country Director, said: “We are looking for ways to equip technology to enhance the safety of both passengers and driving partners. At the end of the trip, both the passenger and the driving partner can evaluate the travel experience. "If a party evaluates a bad experience, we have an automatic alert that notifies our safety and quality team to investigate further."
Features of the new program are in addition to the many existing safety features that Grab has already implemented, such as training and retraining for driving partners, monitoring of driving patterns, as well as accident insurance. Individual for both passengers and drivers during travel with Grab.
Although only operating in Cambodia for less than two years, Grab has been very successful and a leader in both passenger and driver safety in this highly competitive market. In addition, Grab's passenger safety center in the program is expected to be fully operational by the end of this month.
Ministry of Environment issues guidelines on the management and use of weapons in protected areas under the control of the Ministry of Environment
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Environment has issued guidelines on the management and use of firearms in protected areas under the control of the Ministry of Environment in accordance with the Law on Protected Areas, the Law on the Control of Weapons, Explosives and Ammunition and the Penal Code. Of the Kingdom of Cambodia.
According to Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and current spokesman, the Ministry of Environment has been managing 57 protected areas covering an area of 7,249,024 hectares, including 12 national parks and 20 wildlife sanctuaries. Twelve landscape protection sites, eight multi-use areas, five Ramsar sites, two natural heritage sites and three biodiversity corridors.
In addition, the Ministry of Environment has issued another guideline regarding the use of uniforms, badges, helmets and insignia for park rangers stationed in protected areas in order to improve the efficiency of the use of uniforms in accordance with the regulations. Law.
The following is a notice on the use of uniforms, badges, helmets and insignia for rangers stationed in protected areas and instructions on the management and use of weapons in protected areas.
Senior official of the National Committee for Disaster Management calls on philanthropists to help flood victims
(Tbong Khmum): Senior Minister Kun Kim, First Vice Chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management, called on philanthropists, national working groups, especially the Cambodian Red Cross, to provide charitable assistance to people affected by the Mekong River floods.
According to Mr. Kun Kim, the first Chiro commune was severely flooded, with some houses flooded to the floor, some to the floor, some to the beams and some to the roof. Mr. Kun Kim said that in the first Chiro commune, a total of 1,200 families were affected and 700 families were evacuated to safety and are preparing to set up tents for temporary accommodation.
With this visit, Senior Minister Kun Kim also assigned the local authorities, who are all disaster management officers, to help evacuate and solve the problems of the affected people.
"As the Disaster Management Authority, we have made every effort to address the initial problems of the people, to evacuate them so that their lives are not endangered." "According to the order of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management, we have tried our best to help them." Mr. Kun Kim confirmed.
However, the people affected by the floods are struggling to make ends meet, lacking food and other necessities. Senior Minister Kun Kim called on philanthropists, national teams, especially the Red Cross, to provide food and other supplies to help people affected by the Mekong River floods in Tbong Khmum and other provinces affected by the floods. The water of the Mekong River.
Mr. Kun Kim appealed, "I would like to appeal to the philanthropists, national teams, especially the Cambodian Red Cross, a humanitarian organization, to help our people who are suffering from the flooding of the Mekong River." "They are struggling to make ends meet."
On the morning of September 10, Mr. Kun Kim was seen besides distributing expenses and inspecting the impact of flood victims in Tbong Khmum province, and then he went to find a way to download the port. Tourists also float on the Koh Pen Bridge to avoid damaging the bridge.
