China warns foreigners not to intervene after protesters storm US consulate, asks Trump to help liberate Hong Kong
(Hong Kong): The Hong Kong government has issued a warning against foreign intervention after protesters just marched on the US consulate and called for help from Washington. This is according to a quote from CNA on Monday evening, September 9, 2019.
A spokesman for the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (SAR) on Monday warned against foreign interference in the Hong Kong issue, as protesters gathered in front of the US Consulate in Hong Kong on Sunday to support the US Congress passing a law. Human rights issues in Hong Kong and asked US President Donald Trump to help liberate the Chinese autonomous city.
The Hong Kong city government also condemned the extremist actions of protesters who vandalized public property, set fire to and closed lanes at a train station yesterday, marking the 14th week of protests in Hong Kong.
After protesters marched on the United States for help, Chinese government media outlets on Monday warned that Hong Kong was "absolutely inseparable" territory from China and wanted to divide the state. Any form will be destroyed without tolerance.
Heng Sour, spokesman for the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, who led the delegation to the 15th Annual Meeting of the EPS Conference in Korea with other 15 countries recently, claimed that the best of both worlds Cambodia in providing these services include:
1: The minimum contract expiration date until departure to Korea is 41 days, while the average duration of other countries is 48 days.
2: The lowest rate of workers changing employers in the first 3 months after employment.
3. The rate of illegal workers is less than the average.
5: Has the largest computer proficiency test system for Korean language.
6. There is a escort service until the employer comes to pick you up, which is why the employer is never upset about missing a flight or getting lost like some countries.
8. There is a high success rate in life among workers who terminate their employment contracts and return to Cambodia.
Every year, South Korea invites representatives from the 16 countries that send workers to Korea to a meeting to evaluate and rate them, and the best-performing countries will maintain their quotas. In accepting workers from that country and so on.
According to a report provided by the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, as of August 2019, Cambodia had about 54,319 workers working in South Korea, including 11,720 women. The workers send about $ 500 million a year to their families in their hometowns in Cambodia. Mr. Hun Many congratulates the successful candidates and encourages the unsuccessful ones to work harder
