Will repealing the extradition law ease tensions or create more unrest?
In April, the Hong Kong government unveiled a draft law related to the extradition of suspects to mainland China. Critics say the law will not do justice to the suspects until protests erupted in May demanding the Carrie Lam government repeal it. Lam decided to suspend the law in July and announced that it would be repealed on Wednesday.
The repeal of the extradition law was one of the five demands of Hong Kong protesters. These five proposals are:
However, supporters of the Beijing government, including Hong Kong lawmaker Luk Chung-hung, said the repeal of the extradition law was a sign of Hong Kong's softness and would not ease tensions in the country. For now, it only encourages protesters to demonstrate more demands of the government.
Faced with more than three months of protests that have wreaked havoc on Hong Kong's economy, Beijing has repeatedly warned of military intervention to resolve the crisis in Hong Kong amid reports that Chinese troops and armored vehicles Several steel units have been deployed along the Hong Kong border.
According to Article 18 of the Basic Law, in the event of war or unrest that could undermine Hong Kong's national unity and security, the Beijing government may declare a state of emergency as well as implement national laws, including intervention. The military, etc. in the cause of national interest.
Moreover, Beijing itself is well aware that the United States may take advantage of it if China falls into any conflict or crisis.
