Ms. Tek Reth Kamrong: Cambodia is ready to implement cross-border trade facilitation without paperwork
The Secretary of State made the remarks while presiding over the opening of a consultative workshop on cross-border trade facilitation in Cambodia at the Sunway Hotel on Thursday, September 5, 2019 in the presence of senior officials. Royal Government of Cambodia, inter-ministerial officials, representatives of development partners and the private sector, about 50 people.
On that occasion, Ms. Tek Reth Kamrong highlighted the potential and importance of cross-border trade facilitation without paperwork for the development of Cambodia's digital economy and promoting the efficiency, accountability and transparency of trade flows in the region. Create harmony for the world in the long run.
The Secretary of State also did not forget to inform the ceremony about the Royal Government of Cambodia's commitment to boost cross-border trade through administrative procedures, modernization of ASYCUDA customs automation system, introduction of reforms and facilitation measures. Sharp trade, updating and launching the Trade Integration Strategy (CTIS) 2019-2013 and drafting a law on e-commerce.
In addition, Ms. Tek Reth Kamrong also called on development partners and inter-ministerial officials to use today's forum to revisit and revise the study report compiled by UNESCAP on draft activities for cross-border trade without paper. Mark in Cambodia to develop a short-term priority plan to mobilize resources, especially Aid-for-trade to support future implementation.
It should be recalled that on August 29, 2017, Cambodia signed the framework agreement on cross-border trade facilitation in the Asia-Pacific to strengthen effective and transparent international trade cooperation and ensure Achieve legal norms that will bring long-term benefits to countries in the region.
