According to the Korea Automobile Importers and Distributors Association (KAIDA), the number of Japanese vehicles sold in South Korea in August was 1,398, down 56.9 percent year on year. The same month last year, there were 3,247 units. Sales of Japanese-model cars have been falling since July, when the Japanese boycott campaign began in South Korea.
The campaign began when the Tokyo government tightened exports of key technological raw materials to South Korea. In August, Japan formally withdrew South Korea from its trade preferences, while Seoul issued a similar response.
