Minister Ouk Rabun: Ministry of Rural Development issues ID cards to 146 of 455 indigenous communities
(Rattanakiri): Dr. Ouk Rabun, Minister of Rural Development, on September 4, 2019, stated that the Ministry will continue to work hard to promote development work for indigenous peoples in accordance with the Rectangular Strategy Phase 4, Phase 6 of The National Assembly will bring about real change and progress in terms of livelihoods, sustainable natural resource conservation and economic development in the region.
This statement was made on the occasion of Dr. Ouk Rabun presiding over the "25th International Day of Indigenous Peoples", which is celebrated annually by the Ministry of Rural Development. This day was held at the Department of Culture and Fine Arts of Ratanakkiri province this morning, September 4, 2019.
In the interest of indigenous representatives, national organizations and representatives of the Office of the High Commissioner for Human Rights in Cambodia have all confirmed the Royal Government's concern for the development and conservation of indigenous peoples, as well as The Royal Government should provide additional budgets to various ministries to protect and promote the culture, traditions and rights of indigenous peoples in Cambodia.
On that occasion, Dr. Ouk Rabun brought a message from Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia and Samdech Kittiprittbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian Red Cross to the people throughout Ratanakkiri, as well as ethnic people. Indigenous peoples throughout Cambodia, informed of the Royal Government's efforts to indigenous peoples since the 1980s and 1990s.
The Minister stated that the Royal Government has continued to improve the living standards of indigenous peoples as well as the people throughout the country through development in all areas, including some areas under the responsibility of the Ministry of Rural Development, such as: Building rural road infrastructure, rural water supply and sanitation, basic health care, rural credit, to provide better living conditions for people in rural areas.
At present, the Ministry of Rural Development has issued certificates of identity to 146 indigenous communities out of a total of 455 indigenous communities. Separately, the Ministry of Interior has issued certificates of recognition to 139 ethnic minority communities as legal entities, of which 24 communities have already received collective land titles from the Ministry of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction.
