Four Ministries Receive Training on "Rules and Regulations on Public Procurement" from the Ministry of Economy
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Economy and Finance this morning, August 2, 2019, organized the opening ceremony of the training course on "Rules and Regulations on Public Procurement" for 156 procurement management officers from 4 ministries, including Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, Ministry of Public Works and Transport, Ministry of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction and Ministry of Commerce.
This training aims to strengthen, expand capacity and raise awareness to participate in the implementation of the third phase of public financial management reform to achieve success, efficiency and transparency for national socio-economic development.
The opening ceremony was presided over by Dr. Hean Sahip, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Economy and Finance and Chairman of the Capacity Building Working Group of the Ministry of Economy and Finance. This training course lasts for 3 days from September 2 to 4, 2019.
Speaking at the ceremony, Dr. Hean Sahip confirmed that the Public Management Reform Program was officially launched in 2004 by the Royal Government of Cambodia and achieved significant achievements, especially the credibility. Of the budget in the first phase and financial accountability in the second phase.
Currently, the implementation of public financial management reform is in the third phase, aimed at increasing the linkage of the budget to the priority policies of the Royal Government, which will be completed by 2020, before the implementation of the fourth phase. 2021-2025) The goal is performance accountability.
In this direction, the strategic document of the budget reform system 2018-2025, which is the core of the public financial management reform program, was approved by the Royal Government in 2018. The aim of this strategy is to gradually transform the Cambodian budget system into an efficient, accountable and transparent system in line with international best practice.
The overall goal of capacity building of civil servants on public procurement skills is to strengthen and expand the professional capacity of procurement officers in response to the requirements of the performance of roles and responsibilities related to public procurement work, and this capacity building will continue. From 2019 onwards.
He added that the Ministry of Economy and Finance has always paid attention to the training and capacity building of civil servants, which is the key to success in implementing public financial management reforms.
