Road and bridge construction as well as rehabilitation of those roads mostly use foreign loans. Seeing that most roads and bridges are built using foreign loans leads to the question of whether Cambodia does not have a national budget for road construction or just borrows from friendly countries and development partners like this?
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, in an interview with Fresh News, said that Cambodia's national budget needs to address a number of issues, such as the payment of salaries to civil servants, the armed forces, and the construction of schools and departments. Doctors will be ready to solve other problems, which is why Cambodia does not use the national budget to build infrastructure, including roads and bridges.
"The loan management is very well managed by the Ministry of Economy and Finance," he said. We borrow money to build roads, build bridges, not borrow money to buy new cars, buy clothes or spend anything else, but dare to borrow to build infrastructure, roads, bridges to make our economy. "Progress."
Mr. Sun Chanthol said that borrowing foreign money to build this road is like an investment. He explained that if they borrow money to build roads and bridges and make a profit for the national economy, they should dare to borrow.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol also explained that the fact that friendly countries as well as development partners dare to lend money to Cambodia is because they see the possibility of Cambodia can earn interest repayment through economic growth after the construction of infrastructure. He stressed that if they see that Cambodia is not able to repay interest, they will not lend.
According to the Ministry of Economy and Finance, by the end of 2018, Cambodia will have about 7 billion US dollars in foreign debt. Most of that debt is for building infrastructure, such as roads and bridges. 169 Cambodian students ready to go to Israel to absorb agricultural knowledge
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, on the afternoon of August 30, 2019, presided over a get-together with students who will go on an internship for the 12th generation of the 2019-2020 academic year. 169 in Israel for 11 months.
