Royal Government Introduces Three Urgent Measures to Solve Wastewater Problems in Preah Sihanouk Province
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia, through the Ministry of Economy and Finance, has launched three emergency measures to address the problem of wastewater in Sihanoukville. This is according to the press release of the Ministry of Economy, which was received by Fresh News on the morning of August 29, 2019.
These three measures were agreed upon at an inter-ministerial meeting to review and discuss "measures to address infrastructure in Sihanoukville" chaired by Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Economy and Finance and Chairman of the Committee. Economic and Financial Policy (CSF).
The meeting was attended by the Senior Minister, the Minister and senior leaders of the relevant ministries and institutions of the Royal Government, which was held at the Ministry of Economy and Finance.
According to a press release from the Ministry of Economy, in line with the rapid growth of development combined with the high population growth in Sihanoukville, the viability of the province's physical infrastructure is becoming a major challenge for a southwestern province. This.
In fact, in addition to the quality and sustainability issues, the city's roads have also suffered significant damage due to the recent floods with the illegal encroachment on natural streams, which are rainwater harvesting systems. Pour into the sea. In addition, the current drainage and stormwater drainage system can not effectively respond to the amount of rainfall and the discharge of wastewater from the entire city.
In 2018, the amount of wastewater discharged in Sihanoukville will reach 25,000 cubic meters per day and is projected to increase to 47,000 cubic meters per day by 2023 and may continue to increase to 64 , 000 cubic meters per day in 2030. These challenges need to be addressed effectively and urgently to improve the infrastructure system in Sihanoukville.
In response to this issue, the Meeting agreed to put forward emergency measures as follows:
Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth, Deputy Prime Minister and Chairman of the Meeting, thanked the leaders of the relevant Ministries and Institutions for their active participation in discussing solutions and providing input into the master plan for comprehensive infrastructure. Corner and have the correct technical standards and requested all relevant ministries-institutions to continue to provide cooperation with a sense of responsibility and understanding.
