The following is the full text of Keo Maly: The new Chief Representative of KOICA Cambodia visits Choeung Ek Genocide Center
The visit was facilitated by the Cambodian Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts and the Tuol Sleng Genocide Museum (TSGM). The visit is part of the "International Conference on Genocide, Memory and Peace", which runs from today, August 28, 2019 to August 30, 2019.
The Choeung Ek Killing Field Museum, or Choeung Ek, is the site of a former field, field and mass grave of victims of the Khmer Rouge massacre between 1975 and 1979, about 17 km south of Phnom Penh. Cambodia. It is the most famous place known as the "Killing Fields" where the Khmer Rouge regime killed more than a million people between 1975 and 1979.
Please be reminded that this visit was organized within the framework of the "Project on the Preservation and Integration of Tuol Sleng Museum Archives into a Computer System", which was funded by a grant from KOICA.
UNESCO is implementing this project in collaboration with the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts of Cambodia, a project that includes the preservation and digitalization of the archives of the Tuol Sleng Genocide Museum.
These archives are recognized as World Heritage Sites of international importance and are inscribed in the memory of UNESCO, which was registered internationally in 2009.
The project aims to recall those memories through the creation of a website using resources to establish additional links between documents and the creation of multimedia spaces in S-21 administrative documents. Confessions of Tuol Sleng Prisoners (S-21) and people killed there or at the Choeung Ek killing field.
National Minimum Wage Council is negotiating workers' wages in 2020
(Phnom Penh): The National Council for Minimum Wages this morning, August 29, 2019, has been holding discussions on setting the minimum wage in the textile, garment and footwear sector of Cambodia for 2020.
The discussion was held at the Ministry of Labor under the chairmanship of Mr. Ith Sam Heng, Minister of Labor and Vocational Training. The National Assembly of the Cambodian Confederation of Trade Unions and Mr. Ath Thorn, President of the Coalition of Cambodian Garment Workers' Democratic Union (CCAWDU), as well as representatives of employers and other unions.
According to the announcement of the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, the last meeting of the National Council for Minimum Wages to achieve the minimum wage in the textile, garment and footwear sector for 2020 will be held in October 2019. The effective date of the minimum wage will take effect on January 1, 2020.
