Cambodian Students Participate in "Leadership and Social Development Activities" at ASEAN Camp
(Thailand): One of the leading cement manufacturers in ASEAN has organized a multicultural event called "SCG Sharing ASEAN Dreams" from 23 to 26 August 2019 in Thailand with students More than 70 participants from 7 countries: Cambodia, Indonesia, Laos, Myanmar, Philippines, Thailand and Vietnam. This event is designed to provide knowledge and youth development to participating students.
In Rakhine, these ASEAN students not only learn and understand the social development activities that SCG contributes to the livelihoods of local communities, but also directly participate in community activities such as the construction of "fish shelters". Use plastic pipe waste.
For Cambodian student Chhorn Chanpisey, this is the first time she has met with friends from ASEAN countries at such a regional camping event. Ms. Pisey was among 10 students from Cambodia who received scholarships from SCG and attended the five-day camp in Bangkok and Ratchaburi, Thailand.
Samnang said, "This is the first experience for me. I visited Bangkok and saw their amazing infrastructure. And what I like most is the leadership lecture given by the SCG President, a visit to a social activity in Rakhine Province where plastic pipe waste is recycled into fish houses to support the livelihoods of local fishing communities. And to see the banana plantations where SCG helps the families of people with disabilities there.
Students from these seven countries have formed a network across ASEAN for sustainable development in the region. Following the camp, 73 ASEAN students from seven countries also participated in the Regional Conference on Sustainable Development organized by SCG in Bangkok on 26 August 2019.
"The handover of the new S-400 system has begun this morning," Russian President Vladimir Putin told visiting Turkish President Tayyip Erdogan on Tuesday. According to the Turkish Ministry of Defense, the delivery of the second batch of S-400 systems will take about a month to complete.
On July 25, Russia completed the delivery of the first phase of the S-400 system to Turkey, with Ankara accepting the missile defense system in the face of US warnings. Washington has strongly opposed the purchase, saying it would go against NATO military strategy, of which Turkey is a member.
