Dr. Sok Touch's statement came after LICADHO and STT released a report on August 7, 2019, stating that the level of debt of the Cambodian people has increased. This can lead to many human rights violations, including forced land sales, child labor exploitation, and debt migration.
LICADHO and STT found that 2.382 million Cambodians borrowed more than $ 8 billion from microfinance institutions, and the study was conducted in 10 communes in four provinces, including cities. Phnom Penh as well. This is according to a report by human rights group Licadho and STT entitled "Damage to collateral."
According to the President of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, the statements made by human rights group Licadho and STT are based on political rather than social grounds. He asked the two civil society groups to conduct a scientific study before publishing any report.
The Cambodian Microfinance Association (CMA) has also issued a statement on LICADHO and STT distortion reports. The association stated that after a preliminary examination of the report of LICADHO and STT under the heading "Damage to mortgaged property", it was found that this report was a complete distortion of the implementation. Provides loans to microfinance institutions (MFIs) in Cambodia.
